ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [
  "The union-bound P(remove|L)=1-(1-q)^(L-k+1) is elementary combinatorics — for any IID-ish per-position match model, longer strings are more likely to contain >=1 match. This is not an emergent phenomenon; it is the *definition* of a fixed-rate substring filter on variable-length items. Calling it a 'length-biased sampler' restates the closed form.",
  "The score shift Delta>0 under alpha>0 is the trivial consequence of Simpson/selection: condition on a variable (length) correlated with the outcome (difficulty), survivors have shifted outcome mean. No decontamination-specific mechanism is needed — ANY length-dependent filter (truncate long items, subsample by length, even tokenizer drops) produces this.",
  "Delta(alpha) approximately linear (0, +0.009, +0.017, +0.025) is exactly what a first-order coupling p_i = sigmoid(-alpha*g(L)+...) predicts; the 'monotone in alpha' result is mechanically forced by the generative model, not discovered.",
  "Delta(c) growth (+0.004/+0.017/+0.064 at removal 2.6/10.9/36.5%) tracks removal fraction approximately linearly — exactly the expected first-order behavior of a selection effect; no super-linear or threshold phenomenon."
]
CONFOUNDS: [
  "g(L_i)=standardized log(L_i) and p_i=sigmoid(-alpha*g+...) HARDWIRE a monotone length->difficulty map. Real benchmark difficulty is multi-factor; the simulation's alpha is not the same object as 'empirical length-difficulty slope on MMLU/GSM8K'. Mapping the sweep onto 'realistic alpha=0.3-0.6 => +0.9-1.7 pts' is unjustified without a measured real-data slope.",
  "Bernoulli(q)-per-position k-gram membership treats k-gram presence as IID; real web corpora have heavy-tailed n-gram frequencies (Zipf), so removal at fixed mean q will be dominated by a few very-common n-grams, plausibly DECOUPLING removal from length more than the simulation suggests. The union-bound is an upper bound under positive correlation of matches within an item.",
  "Vocab Zipf s=1.07, |V|=5000, lognormal lengths are toy; q is a tunable knob not estimated from any real (benchmark, corpus) pair, so 'med c, k=13' has no anchor to production reality.",
  "alpha=0 control proves the filter is label-blind in *this generative model* — it does not rule out that on real data the filter's selection correlates with difficulty through a third variable (topic, domain, formatting) not captured by L alone."
]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [
  "Real per-benchmark length<->difficulty slope sign and magnitude (the only thing that turns the structural shift into score inflation) — packet acknowledges as residual but no measurement provided; without it, the +0.9-1.7pt headline is a simulation artifact of chosen alpha.",
  "Independence of per-k-gram match events within an item (used in union-bound match): violated by repeated tokens, templated phrasings, named entities.",
  "Bordt 2410.03249's treatment — packet claims Bordt saw the removal but called it benign; novelty rests on this being a genuine delta. No direct quote/citation of Bordt's exact framing in the packet to adjudicate. Without verifying Bordt did NOT model the length-difficulty selection mechanism, the contribution may be a reframing, not a new result.",
  "That 'persists at k=25 with Delta=+0.011' is meaningful — at k=25 removal is only 5%, Delta is ~1pt, within the range of typical eval noise; the 'persists' claim conflates statistical significance (8 seeds, M=4000) with practical significance on real benchmarks where eval-set sizes and seed-to-seed model variance are very different."
]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [
  "n=8 seeds/cell is thin for CI claims especially at small Delta (~0.001 at alpha=0); the alpha=0 'CI covers 0' result has limited power — quantify the MDE at alpha=0: with sigma~0.001 and n=8, what Delta could have been missed?",
  "CIs reported as +/- half-widths with no stated method (t-based? bootstrap? cluster on what?); 36 cells with implicit multiple comparisons — no correction reported.",
  "MAE=0.012 for union-bound fit is reported as aggregate; needs per-cell breakdown and a comparison to a length-independent null (e.g. constant removal rate) to demonstrate the union-bound specifically fits better than 'removal is just ~constant per item'.",
  "No power analysis for the k=25 cell where Delta=+0.011 is barely above the alpha=0 max|Delta|=0.00109 by ~10x — fine, but the gap is not characterized as effect-size / noise-floor ratio across cells.",
  "Headline 'Delta ~+0.9 to +1.7 acc pts' is a units conversion from probability-Delta to 'accuracy points' — the mapping to real benchmark accuracy variance (which is typically 0.5-2pt seed-to-seed for full eval reruns) is not done; the practical-significance argument is undersupported."
]
RATIONALE: The structural anchor (removal monotone in L) and the alpha=0 control are clean and the experiment is honestly pre-registered — these earn the result above RED. But the entire claim of NOVELTY rides on (a) the real length<->difficulty slope on benchmarks that matter being non-trivial and (b) Bordt 2410.03249 not having modeled this selection-effect mechanism — both packet-acknowledged but unverified. The simulation result is mathematically forced once you assume alpha>0 and a fixed-rate substring filter, so the contribution is empirical demonstration of a near-tautological mechanism; without an L1 measurement of real-data alpha or a direct Bordt-delta comparison, +0.9-1.7pt is a what-if not a finding. The k=25, +0.011 'persists' claim is the weakest leg — practically within noise of real eval reruns. YELLOW: result HELD as stated, but the leap from 'simulation supports the mechanism' to 'this matters for real decontam practice' needs the L1 confirmatory pass before publication-grade claims.
